  "summary": "A regional style of American Impressionism centered in Cape Ann, Massachusetts, characterized by bright colors, strong compositions, and maritime subjects.",
  "description": "Distinguished by vibrant colors, bold brushwork, and emphasis on light and shadow in coastal settings; developed by artists who painted en plein air in the fishing villages and rocky landscapes of Cape Ann.",
  "culturalContext": "The school was founded in 1906  by Geoffrey Bing of Rossall School and Keble College, Oxford, with the original aim to \"prepare boys for the Public Schools and the Royal Naval College, Dartmouth\". Bing was 29 and had taught previously in St George's, Broadstairs and at St Andrew's School, Southborough, Tunbridge Wells. Rockport began with only four boys, an assistant master and a matron but quickly grew in size. The school now has around 300 pupils and accepts both boys and girls from the age of 3 until 18 (A Level) as day or boarding pupils.",
